{"The constructor in AccessTokenContext validates `grantType` twice but never checks `rawTokenId`. Line 73 calls `Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, \"Null rawTokenId not allowed\")` instead of `Objects.requireNonNull(rawTokenId, \"Null rawTokenId not allowed\")`, allowing `rawTokenId == null` to slip through.": 0.9881733059883118, "`isAccessTokenId` returns `false` when the grant shortcut matches, causing `expectCodeToToken` to reject valid access-token IDs. The negation in `items[0].substring(3, 5).equals(expectedGrantShortcut)` is inverted \u2014 it should return `true` when the shortcuts match, not `false`.": 0.978877604007721, "Adding the abstract method `getShortcut()` to `OAuth2GrantTypeFactory` breaks binary compatibility with existing grant type implementations. When `DefaultTokenContextEncoderProviderFactory.getGrantTypeByShortcut()` calls `factory.getShortcut()` on a provider compiled against the previous interface, the JVM throws `AbstractMethodError` at runtime. Should add a default method to preserve backward compatibility.": 0.8852609395980835, "The lazy refresh path in `getShortcutByGrantType()` unconditionally overwrites `grantsByShortcuts`/`grantsToShortcuts` when a new grant factory is discovered, bypassing the duplicate-shortcut validation from `postInit()`. If a grant provider deploys post-startup with a shortcut already in use, the old mapping is silently replaced, causing tokens previously issued with that shortcut to decode to the wrong grant type.": 0.7979693412780762}